      "id": "hackernews-2026-05-27-warm-up-your-macbook-2019",
      "title": "Warm up your MacBook (2019)",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://z3ugma.github.io/2019/11/18/warm-up-your-macbook/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48300342\nPoints: 94\n# Comments: 97",
      "body": "18 Nov 2019   You’ve been there - after putting your backpack in a frigid car, walking against the Wisconsin wind, or biking across the frozen lake, you arrive at work. You rest your palms on the keyboard to begin typing your password, and recoil in pain from the sudden cold of the metal sucking the heat from your skin.\nHow do you quickly warm up a laptop? Make it do a lot of work.\nHere’s a one-line, built-in command that will peg your CPU to 100%:\nRun that from a Terminal, and don’t forget about it heh. What the command does is repeatedly send the word yes over and over to the null device, using 100% CPU.\nIf you want to stress your Mac more quickly and get your CPU hotter, try the stress utility:\nstress -c 6 -m 2 -t 300\nThis will start 6 threads that each peg your CPU to 100% and 2 thread that do memory malloc/free. It has a 300-second timeout (5 mins) in case you walk away from your computer so it doesn’t overheat.\nBonus points, you can add an alias to your ~/.bash-profile to automate these options:\nalias warm='stress -c 6 -m 2 -t 300'\nstress: info: [65121] dispatching hogs: 6 cpu, 0 io, 2 vm, 0 hdd",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "kristianp",
      "publishedAt": "2026-05-27T20:42:01.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "/images/frozen-mac-610x371.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://z3ugma.github.io/2019/11/18/warm-up-your-macbook/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
